{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"jq -s '[.[] | {source_id,title,summary}]' /Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/example_site/spaces/mind/sources/records/*.json\"","aggregated_output":"[\n  {\n    \"source_id\": \"source-01-an-information-integration-theory-of-consciousness-w2114900706--09db8f102f39\",\n    \"title\": \"01-an-information-integration-theory-of-consciousness--w2114900706\",\n    \"summary\": \"Tononi’s 2004 article argues that consciousness is identical with a system’s capacity to integrate information. The theory defines Φ as effective information across a complex’s minimum information bipartition, uses complexes to delimit subjects of experience, and uses effective-information relationships to explain the quality of experience. It applies the framework to thalamocortical organization, modular cerebellar-like systems, afferent/efferent pathways, arousal systems, split-brain cases, sleep, seizures, and the possibility of graded consciousness in animals, infants, and artifacts.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"source_id\": \"source-02-mindfulness-training-modifies-subsystems-of-attention-w2132002500--696402dc8f78\",\n    \"title\": \"02-mindfulness-training-modifies-subsystems-of-attention--w2132002500\",\n    \"summary\": \"Jha, Krompinger, and Baime report that mindfulness training changes ANT-measured attention subsystems in different ways depending on training history. Novice MBSR participants showed improved orienting after an 8-week course, experienced retreat participants showed altered alerting and faster no-cue detection after a 1-month retreat, and experienced meditators already showed reduced conflict interference at baseline. The findings are behavioral and preliminary, with important limits from group size, prior-experience differences, task repetition, and lack of active-training controls.\"\n  }\n]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
